{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/943879-making-spirits-more-interesting-to-fight","id":943879,"title":"Making spirits more interesting to fight","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-08-04T14:28:36-07:00","id":943879,"title":"Making spirits more interesting to fight","content":"Have people experimented with using different multi attack rules for spirits? Split dicepools are ok for the smaller guys, but for the big fellows... <br /> Should hunter spiders be using firearms rules? <br /> Does a furmling use book of the wyrm's balefire rules? (they're horrific) Similarly, would mastery of fire resist fire blast? Would a toxin elemental's flood charm poison everything (To not say of the terrors should A fire elemental ever have flood)<br /> What do you all use for calcify being too strong, because logically it's often a one hit kill. Take from dex. It's doomed. Currently I do a 'It must reduce your physicals to 1, then it can fully calcify you' which works pretty well. <br /> Should spirits suffer wound penalties/suffer from damaged limbs and the like? <br /> Currently I've tried interesting combinations like updraft with blast/spear throwing, and umbraquake (which has questionable rules) with anything else. But splitting actions and huge health pools doesn't make for interesting fights.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7244-innocent-flower","name":"Innocent Flower","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7244_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"322"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-08T08:57:42-07:00","id":946199,"title":"","content":"I just use revised multi action rules. And I let spirits have items like weapons and armor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-08T09:16:07-07:00","id":946211,"title":"","content":"I give spirits actions equal to their rank. Gafflings get 1, Jagglings get 2, Lesser Incarna/Totems/Avatars generally get 3, Greater Incarna and Celestines do what they want.<br /> <br /> Depending on how a spirit appears in the umbra, I do let players perform pack tactics on them if they make sense. You could gnarl the fur of a Great Beast, but you couldn't hamstring a flying storm spirit composed of clouds, gale-force wind and lightning. <br /> <br /> Many of my big bad banes get Umbraquake, which lets them threaten lots of Garou at once.<br /> <br /> Feel free to make up your own charms, too. A lot of banes have charms that make them feel and act in a unique way in combat. I had a Shattered Hart that I needed to beef up for an assault on a Caern, so I gave it a charm that allowed it to gain essence equal to the levels of damage it inflicted (and essence would have to go towards powering charms in the following turn or be wasted).<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3695-the-laughing-stranger","name":"The Laughing Stranger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3695_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1010"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-08T12:57:34-07:00","id":946389,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Laughing Stranger</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/943879-making-spirits-more-interesting-to-fight?p=946211#post946211\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Feel free to make up your own charms, too. A lot of banes have charms that make them feel and act in a unique way in combat. I had a Shattered Hart that I needed to beef up for an assault on a Caern, so I gave it a charm that allowed it to gain essence equal to the levels of damage it inflicted (and essence would have to go towards powering charms in the following turn or be wasted).</div> </div> </div> </div>This is one of the best things I can recommend. The list of spirit charms are meant to be possible abilities, not the definitive list of available powers. I generally limit Gafflings to the &quot;normal&quot; charms but around the Jaggling level spirits should have more unique abilities imo. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}